@group(:collections)
mod Std.Tuple
  ## `use Std.Sigma` supplies the projection globals `.1`/`.2`/`.i` lower to
  ## (`sigma_first`, `sigma_second`, `tproj2`…`tproj8`). Every ordinary module
  ## receives those ambiently — `Std.Sigma` is a whole-module `@prelude` — but
  ## `@prelude opaque type Tuple` below puts THIS module in the prelude-bootstrap
  ## closure, and a bootstrap module is elaborated with no prelude of its own so
  ## it cannot be injected back into one of its own dependencies. The import must
  ## therefore be explicit. `Std.Sigma` imports nothing, so this adds no cycle.
  use Std.Sigma

  ## Honest surface tuples.
  ##
  ## `Tuple(T, U)` is the arity-2 product — a flat BEAM `{a, b}` — and
  ## `Tuple(T1, …, Tn)` (3 ≤ n ≤ 8) are the flat n-ary products. A tuple
  ## literal is written `%[a, b, …]` and projected with `.1`, `.2`, … .
  ##
  ## Arity-2 aliases the non-dependent dependent-pair, so the later
  ## position may depend on the earlier one; the n-ary families are plain
  ## flat products. This module is the honest replacement for the old
  ## `Std.Pair`. The n-ary accessors (`third`, …) land alongside the
  ## n-ary literal surface.
  ##
  ## ## Examples
  ##
  ## ```cure
  ## use Std.Tuple
  ##
  ## first(%[1, 2])          # => 1
  ## second(%[1, 2])         # => 2
  ## swap(%[1, 2])           # => %[2, 1]
  ## third(%[1, 2, 3])       # => 3
  ## ```

  ## A BEAM tuple whose arity and component types are NOT known — the bare
  ## `Tuple`, written with no arguments.
  ##
  ## This is the FFI carrier, not a product type: it is what an untyped BEAM
  ## tuple crossing an `@extern` boundary is, and the only thing you can do with
  ## one is hand it back across a boundary that expects it. `:rand`'s seed state
  ## and OTP's `start_link` result (`{ok, Pid}` / `{error, Reason}`) are the
  ## motivating cases — a shape the BEAM fixes and Cure cannot yet name.
  ##
  ## It is nominal and constructor-less for the same reason `Std.Otp.Raw`'s
  ## `RawTerm` is: nothing in Cure may introduce one, so it cannot be confused
  ## with a typed product. Applied `Tuple(T, U)` is a different thing entirely —
  ## the honest Σ/flat product below — and the surface reads the applied form
  ## syntactically, so the two never compete for the name.
  ##
  ## `@prelude` because the untyped form is what the `actor`/`fsm`/`supervisor`
  ## macros emit as their generated `start_link` return type, in modules that
  ## never wrote `use Std.Tuple`.
  @prelude
  opaque type Tuple

  ## The first component of a pair.
  fn first(t: Tuple(a, b)) -> a = t.1

  ## The second component of a pair.
  fn second(t: Tuple(a, b)) -> b = t.2

  ## Swap the two components of a pair.
  fn swap(t: Tuple(a, b)) -> Tuple(b, a) = %[t.2, t.1]

  ## The third component of a 3-tuple.
  fn third(t: Tuple(a, b, c)) -> c = t.3
